-- title:
--   A predicate avoiding every prime yields a finite unit-ideal family
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative ring (in a universe $u$) and let $P$ be an arbitrary predicate on the elements of $B$. Assume that $P$ is satisfied outside every prime: for each prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $B$ there exists $f \in B$ with $f \notin \mathfrak p$ and $P(f)$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a finite index type $\iota$, living in the same universe $u$ as $B$, and a family $f : \iota \to B$ such that the ideal generated by the range of $f$ is the whole of $B$ (the unit ideal, $\top$), and $P(f_i)$ holds for every $i \in \iota$. Thus finitely many elements satisfying $P$ already generate the unit ideal. No finiteness, Noetherianity or nontriviality hypothesis is imposed on $B$; in particular, for the zero ring the hypothesis is vacuous and an empty family may be returned, and the index type is produced as a subtype of $B$ rather than as a `Fin n`.
--
--   This is the algebraic form of the statement that the basic open sets $D(f)$ with $P(f)$ cover $\operatorname{Spec} B$ and that $\operatorname{Spec} B$ is quasi-compact, so that a finite subcover exists; the arbitrary predicate $P$ is intended to be instantiated by 'the desired datum exists over the localisation of $B$ at $f$'. It is used in the Mumford-gluing part of the Čerednik–Drinfeld material, by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.MumfordGlueCore.exists_finite_cover_isPullback_of_zeta_comp_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.MumfordGlueCore.exists_finite_cover_isPullback_of_zeta_comp_eq), to extract a finite affine cover from local existence statements.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_exists_finite_span_range_eq_top_of_forall_isPrime_exists_not_mem.lean

